==English==

===Etymology===
Attested since 1727. Borrowed from {{bor|en|fr|accoucheur}}, from {{m|fr|accoucher||to go to childbed, be delivered}}, from {{der|en|fro|culcher||to lie}}, from {{der|en|la|collocō||I place, put, set in order, assign}}, from {{m|la|con-}} + {{m|la|locō|t=I put, place, set}}. See {{m|en|accouchement}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˌæ.ku.ˈʃɝ/|a=GA}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˌæ.ku.ˈʃɜː/|a=RP}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-accoucheur.wav|Audio (Southern England)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|medicine}} A person, especially a man, who [[deliver]]s a baby (in childbirth).
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1951|month=February|author=Forrest H. Howard|title=The Physiologic Position for Delivery|journal=Northwest Medicine|volume=50|issue=2|location=Portland, Ore.|publisher=Northwest Medical Publishing Association|page=98|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/northwestmedicin5019nort/page/98/mode/2up?q=coucheurs|passage=And lastly, obstetrical chairs seemed most natural to the '''accoucheurs''' of the middle ages.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1992|author=Hilary Mantel|title=A Place of Greater Safety|publisher=Harper Perennial|year_published=2007|page=163|passage=Family story: on the day of his birth the '''accoucheur''' approached his father, the baby wrapped in a cloth.}}

